WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.384
- That was our band logo,

00:00:01.384 --> 00:00:02.881
"The more you drink, the better we sound."

00:00:02.881 --> 00:00:04.040
(film cast and crew laughing)

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:06.496
So here it's, "The more you
drink, the better we talk."

00:00:06.496 --> 00:00:09.829
(upbeat exciting music)

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:14.880
- Hey, everyone.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.400
My name is Ruben Garcia,

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.630
and I am so excited to welcome you

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:19.950
to this special conversation

00:00:19.950 --> 00:00:21.510
celebrating "Father of the Bride."

00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:24.140
Tonight, we'll be joined by
the incredibly talented cast

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.520
and creative team from the
film, to discuss the process

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.530
of bringing this modern rom-com to life.

00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:30.600
It really is a perfect re-imagining

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.100
of a timeless classic,
with a cultural twist.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.450
And it has something for everyone.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.740
Family, music, food,
a little bit of drama,

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.120
and a whole lot of heart.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.480
I really can't wait for
you all to check it out.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:43.910
Before we jump into the conversation,

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.713
here is a quick look at the film.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:47.793
- Poor guy.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:50.890
- Poor guy?

00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:52.230
Okay?
- I just feel sorry

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:53.360
for someone who has no choice

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.230
but to fight for money you know?

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:57.415
- Would you like me to change the channel?

00:00:57.415 --> 00:00:58.520
- No, no, no.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:00:59.997
This is your house, please-
- No, no, no-

00:00:59.997 --> 00:01:02.470
No, (chuckles) What sport
would you prefer to watch?

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:04.230
- I don't really watch sports.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.280
- You don't watch sports?

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:07.390
- No.
- Do you play sports?

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:08.730
- I like hiking.

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:10.200
- Hiking's not a sport.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.650
Your father never taught you any sports?

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:12.950
He never took you to any games?

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:14.260
- No, he did, he has a soccer team,

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:15.820
and we had to go watch him play every week

00:01:15.820 --> 00:01:17.426
until I got tired of it.
- Hello men!

00:01:17.426 --> 00:01:20.380
(speaking in foreign language)
(light jazzy music)

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:21.213
- Adan, your parents will be
- A soccer team-

00:01:21.213 --> 00:01:22.890
- Here soon, right?
- Yeah.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:24.620
- I need to warm up the fricassee.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:25.742
- Let me help you.

00:01:25.742 --> 00:01:27.120
(Sofia speaking in foreign language)

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.800
- He got him right in the bun.
- Yeah.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.000
- Welcome to Academy Panels.

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.350
My name is Shawn Finnie,
Executive Vice President,

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:35.030
and today I have the honor and privilege

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:36.890
of sitting down with the amazing cast

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.340
and filmmakers from the
"Father of the Bride."

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.810
Welcome, everybody!
- (in unison) Thank you Shawn!

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:42.740
- Y'all gotta make a little more noise.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:43.631
Welcome, everybody!

00:01:43.631 --> 00:01:46.120
(group cheering and clapping)
(Shawn laughing)

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:47.560
Honestly y'all, I'm so excited

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.680
to see this re-imagined story told

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.950
with such culture, such truth and honesty.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.180
And it's really the documentation

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:54.500
of like the human story, right?

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.710
We all know how interesting
weddings can be,

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:00.250
we all know the drama it
brings out in families,

00:02:00.250 --> 00:02:01.830
and all of the others,
and I just appreciate

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:03.110
the way that you all approached it.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:04.770
And Gaz I want to start with you,

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:05.780
because I feel like we've seen

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.730
this timeless classic re-imagined before.

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:11.280
We had it in the '50s, we had
it re-imagined in the '90s,

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.130
and now it has such an interesting lens,

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:14.710
because I feel like it
represents the world

00:02:14.710 --> 00:02:16.630
that we all live in, and
I'm just curious for you,

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:19.190
what brought you to this
film, and wanting to tell

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:21.780
this story specifically,
"Father of the Bride?"

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:22.980
- So,

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.440
when I read the script,
what clicked with me

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:27.630
is this

00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:28.463
sense,

00:02:28.463 --> 00:02:29.990
it's this instinct

00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:33.090
that I'm an immigrant into this country,

00:02:33.090 --> 00:02:36.770
and now, my daughter when
she's with her friends,

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:39.731
she comes in speaking
like a Valley girl saying,

00:02:39.731 --> 00:02:40.770
(mimicking)
"Daddy?"

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.890
And I just go like, "What?"

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.167
I'm like, "Call me Papi or something,

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:47.834
"but don't do that," no?

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:50.990
And you kind of realize
that, we came to this country

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:53.210
looking for better opportunities,

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:56.530
but with that comes a certain tax of,

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:58.410
losing,

00:02:58.410 --> 00:02:59.850
your traditions and culture,

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:03.063
and watching that fade into the U.S.,

00:03:03.063 --> 00:03:05.370
into the American melting pot.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.450
And, that was my way in.

00:03:07.450 --> 00:03:09.210
And we spoke about that,

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.340
and everyone said, "That's a way in."

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.250
I spoke about this with Andy,

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:17.050
and Andy immediately
started to just say how much

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:18.840
this was also an issue for him.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:21.260
So we teamed up around that as well.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.380
And of course, Matt's script
had all of that texture

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.220
of Mexicans and Cubans coming together,

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:30.130
and it felt like it was
just gonna be a lot of fun

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:36.960
for the creators to agree on
what are the similarities,

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:40.210
and then what would the differences be,

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:42.770
being that we're the Mexicans.

00:03:42.770 --> 00:03:46.220
Adria is like an emissary
of both countries, no?

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:48.140
And then you have the Cubans,

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:50.140
and like we all,

00:03:50.140 --> 00:03:52.140
spend vacations in Miami.

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:56.840
So then Gloria and Andy
introduced us to their Miami.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.290
And eventually, that was
how we built the movie.

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:02.990
And the fact that, I hoped that
was gonna be the experience,

00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:04.677
and it was even better.
- I love that.

00:04:04.677 --> 00:04:07.110
And I love that it's a
universal film, you know?

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:08.780
It's a universal film, a universal story

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.730
documenting again, the human experience.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:11.730
Matt, coming to you.

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:14.440
I love that we also really
delve into the relationship,

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.070
and the gender kind of roles and changes

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:18.800
where we saw Adria's
character really propose,

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:19.850
which we don't often see.

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:22.160
So I'm curious from the
perspective of writing,

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:24.500
how important was it to be reflective of,

00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.840
this new space that we're
in, talking about gender,

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:30.360
the roles, the generation
and traditional gaps there?

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:31.950
- Gaz and I always talked about, you know,

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.050
the Spencer Tracy version is very much

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:35.870
the "Father of the Bride" of it's time.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:37.030
And the Steve Martin version

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:39.450
is very much the "Father
of the Bride" of it's time.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.810
What is the "Father of
the Bride" of our time?

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:44.370
We knew we had this great immigrant story.

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.620
I've always been interested
in the fact that,

00:04:47.590 --> 00:04:52.020
whether it was the mother of
the bride or the bride herself,

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.690
the previous films, as great as they are,

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:57.800
aren't hugely concerned
with their stories, right?

00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:02.410
Elizabeth Taylor, in the
MGM version from the '50s,

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:06.310
she's all about picking the china,

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:08.810
and you know, that's kind of it.

00:05:08.810 --> 00:05:11.180
And so we were really interested,

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:12.810
maybe because I have two daughters,

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.560
and you know, they're very independent,

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:19.100
I thought the "Father of the
Bride" of our time could,

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:21.980
while remaining true to that
father/daughter relationship

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:24.350
that is obviously at
the core of the movie,

00:05:24.350 --> 00:05:27.690
we could get a first look
that the previous movies

00:05:27.690 --> 00:05:30.070
were not concerned
about into the marriage,

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:31.600
of the parents of the bride,

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:33.960
and where are they in their relationship?

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.820
And in the case of the bride,

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:40.560
this incredibly strong
and independent woman

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:42.210
who,

00:05:42.210 --> 00:05:45.490
is not going to just,
like Elizabeth Taylor,

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:46.700
as much as I love her in that movie,

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:51.660
is not gonna sit idly by while
her father calls the shots.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.950
So that was hugely important.

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:57.830
Like, that felt like a
modern take that honored

00:05:57.830 --> 00:05:59.160
what's great about "Father of the Bride"

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.150
and why people love that movie,
but yet felt of our time.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:04.780
- I love that, and going to the parents,

00:06:04.780 --> 00:06:06.970
you are playing two iconic roles.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:08.450
First of all you're two iconic actors-

00:06:08.450 --> 00:06:09.283
- No.
- Let's just give it up

00:06:09.283 --> 00:06:10.980
to you all first.
- We're just humans-

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:13.623
- Iconic humans, who
happen to be iconic actors.

00:06:13.623 --> 00:06:15.016
- I'm kind of new on the scene.

00:06:15.016 --> 00:06:17.450
He's like, the actor
dude. (group laughing)

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:19.117
- What was it about when
Gaz brought it to you,

00:06:19.117 --> 00:06:20.940
and you mentioned it a little bit before,

00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:24.030
like what was it when he brought
this specific kind of idea

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:26.030
around "Father of the Bride" to you both,

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:29.120
that made you all really just
invested and interested in it?

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:31.640
- Well, the conceit of doing it

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:35.019
was a great privilege,
to be thought of like,

00:06:35.019 --> 00:06:37.897
as Paul presented the idea,
"We're doing this thing based

00:06:37.897 --> 00:06:40.857
"around a Cuban family, and I
wasn't supposed to tell you,

00:06:40.857 --> 00:06:43.210
"but we're writing it for you."

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:44.350
You know, he told me that early on.

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:47.300
I said, "No, I," so I felt
it was a privilege to really,

00:06:47.300 --> 00:06:50.310
to be considered, and I
was honored to jump in.

00:06:50.310 --> 00:06:52.960
And then I read Matt's draft,

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.280
and immediately was taken by it,

00:06:55.280 --> 00:06:56.270
And I felt,

00:06:56.270 --> 00:07:00.210
not only a privilege,
but a responsibility,

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:02.560
to jump in to make sure that

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:04.500
I can contribute in getting it right.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.110
Because a lot of times,
cultures are represented

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.430
in such stereotypical ways

00:07:09.430 --> 00:07:12.630
from people who don't have
an insight really to it,

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:16.270
and, so that was something
that I felt important for me.

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:17.953
I guess at the core of it all,

00:07:18.873 --> 00:07:22.240
there was a discovery that
we have this arc in the movie

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.770
that's unique to this film, and also,

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:29.887
that, as Matt said,
"The women in the piece

00:07:29.887 --> 00:07:32.983
"are the driving force and the catalyst"

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:36.610
for this man to change
and to be able to move on

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:39.480
from the traditions he's grown up with

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.230
that are very important to
him, and they're very real.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:44.100
You know, he's protecting it.

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:45.340
He's like a dog with a bone.

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:48.040
He will not let go, and the more pressure

00:07:48.040 --> 00:07:49.960
you put on a character like that then,

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.480
the more humor, and
more fodder for comedy,

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:58.450
and also for pain, and some
emotional moments to surface.

00:07:58.450 --> 00:08:00.570
I was all in from the get-go.

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:05.250
And then when Gaz came on
board, we started to spit-ball

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:08.530
about who are these people really?

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:09.893
Who are the actors we want?

00:08:10.870 --> 00:08:12.080
Who is the Mexican family?

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:15.799
Because depending who they are,
they could be from Monterey,

00:08:15.799 --> 00:08:18.250
they can be from Mexico
City, are they from Durango?

00:08:18.250 --> 00:08:19.390
Are they a farmer?

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:20.490
Is he a rancher?

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:22.430
We knew he had to have a lot of money,

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:23.263
but how did he come about it?

00:08:23.263 --> 00:08:27.400
'Cause depending on who it
is, you have to cast an actor

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:30.900
that has the persona of
that characterization,

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:32.236
the behavior.
- He's a flirt,

00:08:32.236 --> 00:08:34.850
the father of the groom.
- The father is a flirt.

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:37.600
Yes, he's a Tulum flirt.
- Exactly. (group laughing)

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:38.870
And for me, it was a no-brainer.

00:08:38.870 --> 00:08:40.630
I mean, I get a text from Andy

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:42.097
whose texts are short and sweet.

00:08:42.097 --> 00:08:43.810
"I'm sending you a script."

00:08:43.810 --> 00:08:44.840
Okay?

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:47.910
I know that he doesn't do that, you know,

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:51.130
without really believing in something.

00:08:51.130 --> 00:08:53.240
So I'm thinking, what is it?

00:08:53.240 --> 00:08:55.370
And when it arrives, it's
"Father of the Bride."

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:56.670
I'm going, "Oh my God!"

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:01.077
I was a fan, and I'm going,
"These are big shoes to fill.

00:09:01.077 --> 00:09:02.580
"How is that gonna be?"

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:04.890
I read your script, I laughed out loud.

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:08.470
I mean, you and I share a lot
of the same sense of humor,

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:12.220
that I know we leaned into
more of the heartwarming

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:15.700
and making kind of fun of
ourselves and our culture,

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:17.920
but I love all those jokes
that you had in there,

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:20.250
and it drew me in, and then, Gaz,

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:23.440
I find out he's directing,
and he wanted to talk to me.

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:25.723
And I said, "Okay, am I gonna
read for this, sort of?"

00:09:25.723 --> 00:09:27.510
He says, "No, we want you for the role."

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:29.723
I go, "Damn, they're gonna
trust me?" (group laughing)

00:09:29.723 --> 00:09:31.660
This is my first leading role,

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:35.810
And I had watched (speaking
in foreign language)

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:38.330
maybe like a month before

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:42.290
you reached out, and I fell
in love with that film so much

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:44.900
that I looked for, who is this?

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:45.950
How did they do this?

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:51.400
It was such a beautiful film,
and funny, but heartwarming.

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:53.970
So I was all in from the
get-go, and I thought,

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:55.770
my God, what a great
opportunity, you know?

00:09:55.770 --> 00:09:56.850
I've waited a long time.

00:09:56.850 --> 00:10:00.087
They've sent me scripts
through the years that I go,

00:10:00.087 --> 00:10:03.380
"You know, mm," I carry a lot of baggage

00:10:03.380 --> 00:10:07.290
from my musical career, and
it's a responsibility to me.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.900
I don't want to disappoint,
either my fans or my culture,

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:14.040
and I've always chosen things that,

00:10:14.040 --> 00:10:16.483
like I was saying to
you, elevate the culture,

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:19.460
or advance me as an actor, you know?

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:21.730
So, those things hadn't arrived,

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:23.880
but when this came, it was like ooh-hoo!

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:25.685
I moved my whole schedule around,

00:10:25.685 --> 00:10:27.170
(group laughing)
so that I could do it!

00:10:27.170 --> 00:10:30.020
Because I was really busy,

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:32.720
and I said, "Oh my God, I
can't let this slide by."

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:35.110
And it was such a great experience.

00:10:35.110 --> 00:10:37.400
We had such a good time.
- You know,

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:39.820
both of your individual
contributions to the film,

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:42.092
amazing job by the way.
- Oh, thank you.

00:10:42.092 --> 00:10:43.260
- But I'm just curious, talk to me about

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:45.470
the dynamic of your roles together,

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:47.160
and the intersection of like the family,

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:50.170
bringing Diego's character to the family,

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:51.060
and just your experience

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:53.460
of those two characters coming together.

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:55.630
- I feel like this is when
we lie and tell everyone

00:10:55.630 --> 00:10:59.297
we're method actors or
something. (group laughing)

00:10:59.297 --> 00:11:02.810
- Honestly, it was probably
one of the easiest things

00:11:02.810 --> 00:11:06.380
about this film was
discovering our dynamic.

00:11:06.380 --> 00:11:07.890
We knew each other from before,

00:11:07.890 --> 00:11:11.290
and, we just had like
an instant connection.

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:12.690
I just trusted him.

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:15.870
Sort of the second I
met him we had a dinner

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:18.770
that lasted like about
four and a half hours.

00:11:18.770 --> 00:11:21.710
And we talked about
everything but the movie.

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:23.230
- Yeah.
- We talked about our lives,

00:11:23.230 --> 00:11:24.190
we talked about our secrets,

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:27.847
and we really had just, the best time.

00:11:27.847 --> 00:11:30.000
And I respect him as an
actor, he respects me,

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.360
and since day one, I think,
I knew that I trusted him

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:35.500
and that I was in really good hands.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:40.140
It allowed me to sort of just
really settle into Sofie.

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:43.440
And then, discovering
those little differences

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:45.700
between how Sofie was sort of brought up

00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:47.970
and how Adan was brought
up, I think there was a lot

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:51.500
in conversations that I had
with Gaz and with you as well,

00:11:51.500 --> 00:11:53.680
of like, Gaz asked me a
really important question,

00:11:53.680 --> 00:11:56.180
which was, "What do you see in a man?"

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:58.030
Like, what are the qualities in a man

00:12:00.076 --> 00:12:01.810
that attract you to someone?

00:12:01.810 --> 00:12:03.827
And that was an interesting
question from him, and I go,

00:12:03.827 --> 00:12:08.040
"Well, I want someone that
listens and that supports me."

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:11.450
And, then we started building that

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:13.640
into the beginning of the monologue,

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:15.490
and it's the reason for that question

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:17.347
that Gaz smartly asked, and was like,

00:12:17.347 --> 00:12:21.367
"Those are the qualities
that someone like Sofie,

00:12:21.367 --> 00:12:22.960
"a modern woman, should look like."

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:24.733
Sort of like depicting,

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:28.470
or killing the stereotype
of the prince charming

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:29.647
and really making like,

00:12:29.647 --> 00:12:31.540
"Hey, this is the new prince charming."

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:34.050
It's someone who supports
you, that lets you fly,

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:38.250
that allows you to have space,
that sees you as an equal

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:44.780
and doesn't see himself superior
to you or like a housewife.

00:12:44.900 --> 00:12:49.900
Really sees you as an intellectual,
and as a career woman.

00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:53.053
And that's what sort of, we really,

00:12:54.040 --> 00:12:57.910
loved, because that's
who Sofie would, marry.

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:02.000
And I think that was so
easy for us to discover.

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:03.933
I mean, I think Diego is probably,

00:13:05.010 --> 00:13:06.943
one of the biggest
gentlemen I've ever met.

00:13:06.943 --> 00:13:08.040
- Nah-
- So it was so,

00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:10.267
it was just like, even off set I was like,

00:13:10.267 --> 00:13:13.974
"You're such an Adan in so many ways."

00:13:13.974 --> 00:13:14.940
(group laughing)
He really is!

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:16.910
- Yeah, my wife feels the same way!

00:13:16.910 --> 00:13:19.600
(group laughing)

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:21.030
- Well, there's only two types of casting,

00:13:21.030 --> 00:13:24.380
typecasting and miscasting.
- Exactly. (group laughing)

00:13:24.380 --> 00:13:26.550
- But I love that because, and
Diego I'm gonna come to you,

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:28.510
but in the scene where
she's talking about,

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:30.260
when Andy your character
is talking to him,

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:31.590
and you all are having that conversation,

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:34.130
and you realize that the move to Mexico,

00:13:34.130 --> 00:13:36.130
you're really following her.
- Yes!

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:38.140
- And when initially, you thought that

00:13:38.140 --> 00:13:40.890
she was following him, and
so diving into that aspect

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:43.760
of how supportive he is, and
his character is as a husband,

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:45.210
like that he's willing to maneuver

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:47.070
and move his whole life to be around you,

00:13:47.070 --> 00:13:48.960
which is reflective of that modern day,

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:51.220
which is reflective of this
new time that we're in,

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:52.390
which allows this new story

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:53.530
to kinda have a lot more breadth.

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:54.550
But Diego, I wanna come to you

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:57.590
from just your perspective
of your character,

00:13:57.590 --> 00:13:59.940
and how it intersects with Adria.

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:03.150
- I was honored when Gaz
reached out at first,

00:14:03.150 --> 00:14:05.730
and I was very excited 'cause I came from

00:14:05.730 --> 00:14:07.390
doing a project where
the character I played

00:14:07.390 --> 00:14:09.230
was completely different to Adan.

00:14:09.230 --> 00:14:12.063
And, after I read the
script and I understood,

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:16.780
you know, how important it was for Adan

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:19.110
to be completely different to Billy,

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:22.650
and to really contrast that,
and have it be more like

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:27.650
the modern guy who's you know,
supportive of his fiance,

00:14:27.880 --> 00:14:32.440
who's willing to change,
and adapt to her life,

00:14:32.440 --> 00:14:35.270
and I love that 'cause it
changes that stereotype.

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:37.530
And then immediately after, I
was like, "Adria, let's talk."

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:38.470
- Mm-hmm.
- You know?

00:14:38.470 --> 00:14:41.280
And we would just, we
spent hours on the phone

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:43.570
just discussing, going over scenes,

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:45.470
talking about the character, but also,

00:14:45.470 --> 00:14:48.170
mainly I just wanted us
to get to know each other

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:52.010
so that that chemistry showed, on screen.

00:14:52.010 --> 00:14:55.910
You know, like Adria said, we
went out to dinner in Atlanta.

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:58.050
- You took her to yoga,
camping, and surfing.

00:14:58.050 --> 00:14:59.080
- Exactly.
(group laughing)

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:01.494
Exactly, (laughs) exactly!

00:15:01.494 --> 00:15:04.150
We just hit it off, and you
know, once we were on set,

00:15:04.150 --> 00:15:05.410
it was a piece of cake.

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:07.390
So being able to work that

00:15:08.728 --> 00:15:12.620
with Gaz, and talk about
the character with him,

00:15:12.620 --> 00:15:15.360
and understand those differences,

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:20.360
and those subtleties that would
make Adan contrast to Billy,

00:15:20.440 --> 00:15:24.770
and working with Adria together,
and getting that chemistry,

00:15:24.770 --> 00:15:27.300
and just feeling comfortable
and trusting each other

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:28.837
was what was really special about this.

00:15:28.837 --> 00:15:31.530
- And we saw your character
really kind of inspire

00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:34.380
in your character, this
idea of vulnerability,

00:15:34.380 --> 00:15:37.410
in understanding what's
happening in your stories, right?

00:15:37.410 --> 00:15:40.320
'Cause it's about the bride,
but it was about the family.

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:41.960
It was about the other
daughter, it was about

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:43.090
all the different elements.
- Everybody, yeah.

00:15:43.090 --> 00:15:45.800
- So you're watching
the human story unfold,

00:15:45.800 --> 00:15:47.050
but you're watching your marriage

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:48.730
kind of like get on the rocks,

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:50.560
and then we're watching you kind of really

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:52.920
be able to inspire in him this idea.

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:56.993
- To a certain degree I think
that Matt can relate to this.

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:01.390
You're chasing certain concepts,

00:16:01.390 --> 00:16:03.780
and as you're going into production,

00:16:03.780 --> 00:16:05.280
they start to come into focus

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:08.650
when, now you know who your cast is, no?

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:11.160
And during the small conversations

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:14.030
as you're building it out
and interviewing everyone,

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:15.570
information starts to come through

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:19.300
that suddenly create new
layers of specificity,

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:22.590
where abstract thoughts
start to become particular.

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:25.360
So, Adria's story when we were speaking

00:16:25.360 --> 00:16:27.420
the first time about the movie,

00:16:27.420 --> 00:16:31.160
she just tells me the story
of how she tells her dad

00:16:31.160 --> 00:16:34.937
that she's engaged, and
the dad was like, "Okay."

00:16:36.010 --> 00:16:38.930
And suddenly, he disappears,

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:40.990
and they're living in Miami,

00:16:40.990 --> 00:16:42.350
and,

00:16:42.350 --> 00:16:45.227
they call Adria up, and they're like,

00:16:45.227 --> 00:16:46.847
"Your dad is in New York."

00:16:47.860 --> 00:16:49.140
And,

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:50.690
why?

00:16:50.690 --> 00:16:52.180
And she calls, "Dad, what are you doing?"

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:55.743
He's like, "I don't know, I
just felt like seeing a play."

00:16:55.743 --> 00:16:57.920
(group laughing)

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:00.057
- Wow!
- And I was like,

00:17:00.057 --> 00:17:02.730
"That's amazing," and just the stories

00:17:02.730 --> 00:17:04.880
that she kept saying
on how on her wedding,

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:07.363
it was almost as if she had
to take care of her dad.

00:17:07.363 --> 00:17:08.977
I was like,

00:17:08.977 --> 00:17:11.747
"That is particular information

00:17:11.747 --> 00:17:13.820
"that just makes the movie real."

00:17:13.820 --> 00:17:14.653
- Yeah.
- You know?

00:17:14.653 --> 00:17:17.360
And it's all of those
conversations with Diego

00:17:17.360 --> 00:17:20.030
that suddenly he was just talking about,

00:17:20.030 --> 00:17:22.760
like where he was struggling
in understanding the character

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:26.240
and how these ideas could
actually come together,

00:17:26.240 --> 00:17:28.540
that, it turns out that

00:17:28.540 --> 00:17:32.120
Diego has been married
more to the character

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:34.590
that he has been playing
for all these seasons,

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:37.410
and it turns out that
Diego played more himself

00:17:37.410 --> 00:17:40.740
in this movie, than in other characters.

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:44.750
He's like this really
good-natured, sweet guy,

00:17:44.750 --> 00:17:47.610
that he just dared to
show that side of himself.

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:49.500
So it was just those sort of things

00:17:49.500 --> 00:17:51.400
that started to inform the characters,

00:17:51.400 --> 00:17:55.240
and it just made the movie more
intimate for all of us, no?

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:57.330
I mean, Andy, I'm sure you have things

00:17:57.330 --> 00:18:00.367
that you can't talk about (laughs) now,

00:18:00.367 --> 00:18:02.560
aren't you marrying like
two daughters right now?

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:04.720
- Yeah, two weddings in 30 days.

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:06.337
- Two weddings in 30 days?

00:18:06.337 --> 00:18:07.730
- "Father of the Bride" is a
documentary, (group laughing)

00:18:07.730 --> 00:18:10.056
there are not too many facts about Andy!

00:18:10.056 --> 00:18:11.180
- And a press tour in the middle.

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:12.350
That's crazy.

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:13.300
- Light work.

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:14.850
- By the way, I really need to say,

00:18:14.850 --> 00:18:16.700
last night when we watched it,

00:18:16.700 --> 00:18:19.210
I watched it for the first
time on the big screen,

00:18:19.210 --> 00:18:21.993
so many things came to life that,

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:24.110
you,

00:18:24.110 --> 00:18:28.177
even in a simple line,
which got a huge laugh,

00:18:28.177 --> 00:18:29.500
"Do you have tea?"

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:33.640
It's like, in every single little line,

00:18:33.640 --> 00:18:38.380
you brought this,
personality and this person

00:18:38.380 --> 00:18:39.830
to life even more.

00:18:39.830 --> 00:18:41.800
People were cracking up over it because,

00:18:41.800 --> 00:18:43.380
you know immediately like here he is,

00:18:43.380 --> 00:18:46.500
Mr. Espresso man, whipping
it up like you know,

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:49.390
he's so proud of making the Cuban coffee.

00:18:49.390 --> 00:18:51.480
And you just went, "Do you have tea?"

00:18:51.480 --> 00:18:53.163
And, oh my gosh.
- But also,

00:18:54.226 --> 00:18:57.040
it's all informed from
the script, you know,

00:18:57.040 --> 00:18:58.980
before he says, "Do you have tea?"

00:18:58.980 --> 00:19:02.857
He's already said, "I've been proposed to,

00:19:02.857 --> 00:19:06.030
"and I like yoga, camping, and surfing."

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:09.500
So it's all like beats
that continue to create.

00:19:09.500 --> 00:19:11.100
It's like yet another thing for Billy

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:13.097
to go, "You don't like sports?"

00:19:13.097 --> 00:19:13.930
"I like hiking."

00:19:13.930 --> 00:19:16.172
"Hiking's not a sport."
(group laughing)

00:19:16.172 --> 00:19:17.892
- And then he asks like,
"Did you play any sports?"

00:19:17.892 --> 00:19:18.725
He's like, "Not at all."
(group laughing)

00:19:18.725 --> 00:19:20.350
You're like, "Interesting."

00:19:20.350 --> 00:19:22.270
I mean, it is the modern world
- We'll change the channel,

00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:23.970
what do you wanna watch?
- Yeah!

00:19:23.970 --> 00:19:25.923
- The modern woman with the modern man.

00:19:25.923 --> 00:19:26.756
- The Food Network?
- Yeah!

00:19:26.756 --> 00:19:28.065
- Can we put on the Food Network?

00:19:28.065 --> 00:19:29.830
(group laughing)
- HGTV?

00:19:29.830 --> 00:19:33.780
- What do you want individuals
to take away from this film?

00:19:33.780 --> 00:19:38.140
- There's a certain hope that
I feel this film promises,

00:19:38.140 --> 00:19:41.410
where, generations are divided,

00:19:41.410 --> 00:19:43.943
global views are divided, and yet,

00:19:44.840 --> 00:19:47.580
they live under the same household, no?

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:48.413
So,

00:19:48.413 --> 00:19:50.420
I kinda want,

00:19:50.420 --> 00:19:53.810
there's a bit of a utopic thinking

00:19:53.810 --> 00:19:56.590
about what the idyllic
family can look like,

00:19:56.590 --> 00:19:59.330
coming together in spite
of their differences.

00:19:59.330 --> 00:20:00.563
I also think that,

00:20:02.040 --> 00:20:04.840
a lot of media right now is

00:20:04.840 --> 00:20:09.620
trying to portray the most
progressive versions of life,

00:20:09.620 --> 00:20:12.610
and I wanted to for a second,

00:20:12.610 --> 00:20:15.590
portray, the people that are like,

00:20:15.590 --> 00:20:18.490
second or third wave behind the progress.

00:20:18.490 --> 00:20:20.487
Just sort of saying like, "Yeah, I agree,

00:20:20.487 --> 00:20:23.580
"I'm just, let me catch up," you know?

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:25.890
And speak to the middle of the country

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:28.540
that is not polarized,
they're just sort of trying

00:20:28.540 --> 00:20:31.400
to figure out where the new voices are.

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:35.150
And just speak to the families
where all that conflict

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:38.687
is happening, and just say, "You know,

00:20:38.687 --> 00:20:41.563
"it's a generational thing,
It's happening everywhere.

00:20:42.537 --> 00:20:44.547
"Hang in there, it'll be fine.

00:20:44.547 --> 00:20:46.007
"We all want to be together,

00:20:46.007 --> 00:20:48.764
"and we can break bread
at the end," you know?

00:20:48.764 --> 00:20:51.230
That's my hope at least for the movie.

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:53.910
- Which is why it's a universal
film documenting humanity.

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:54.743
- Yeah.
- Right?

00:20:54.743 --> 00:20:56.080
And documenting family, and documenting

00:20:56.080 --> 00:20:57.500
the intersections of all of them

00:20:57.500 --> 00:20:59.260
with this daughter,
with the other daughter.

00:20:59.260 --> 00:21:00.520
Adria, I'm curious for you.

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:02.250
What do you want people
to take from the film?

00:21:02.250 --> 00:21:03.300
- There's so many things I want people,

00:21:03.300 --> 00:21:05.680
I think, to take out of this movie.

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:07.913
I think Gaz said it really beautifully.

00:21:09.180 --> 00:21:10.750
I think this movie, like you just said,

00:21:10.750 --> 00:21:12.090
is incredibly universal,

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:16.710
and I'm excited for people
to see two cultures,

00:21:16.710 --> 00:21:18.700
two Latin American cultures come together,

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:21.920
and to finally see the
differences, and to not,

00:21:21.920 --> 00:21:24.000
we've always sort have
been put in one box,

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.900
and I mean, I've been told multiple times

00:21:26.900 --> 00:21:29.940
that Puerto Rico's in
Mexico or in Argentina.

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:32.890
And, or like I'm Puerto
Rican, that means I'm Mexican.

00:21:32.890 --> 00:21:35.250
And it doesn't, and I think this film

00:21:35.250 --> 00:21:38.010
is gonna show those differences,
and also show the world

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:41.160
that we're just like everybody else.

00:21:41.160 --> 00:21:44.560
I think for the first time
in a really long time,

00:21:44.560 --> 00:21:46.870
we've sort of all taken the reigns

00:21:46.870 --> 00:21:48.750
and we haven't been stereotyped.

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:52.110
And we've taken the reigns
of our narrative yet again,

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:55.170
and we're like, "Hey, guys,
this is actually who we are."

00:21:55.170 --> 00:21:58.443
And it's funny and it's
warm, but at the same time,

00:21:59.289 --> 00:22:01.540
we're just exactly like you.

00:22:01.540 --> 00:22:03.860
We just maybe listen to different music,

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:05.100
and maybe eat different food,

00:22:05.100 --> 00:22:06.690
and maybe dance a little different.

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:09.110
At the same time, we're all
in this human experience.

00:22:09.110 --> 00:22:11.190
So, I want people to take away that.

00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:14.400
To sort of see us, and see us for sort of

00:22:15.650 --> 00:22:18.410
who we are, and just really enjoy

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:20.460
and have a good time watching this film.

00:22:20.460 --> 00:22:22.410
We had a blast making this.

00:22:22.410 --> 00:22:25.020
We had the best time,
and I think it's probably

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:27.610
the best filming experience I've ever had,

00:22:27.610 --> 00:22:31.790
and I think I will ever have,
because I have fallen in love

00:22:31.790 --> 00:22:33.300
with every single

00:22:33.300 --> 00:22:34.460
human

00:22:34.460 --> 00:22:35.580
on this

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:36.540
film,

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:37.850
like genuinely.

00:22:37.850 --> 00:22:42.330
And us actors, I mean, let's
be all really honest right now,

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:44.540
We lie all the time.
(group laughing)

00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:45.373
We lie all the time!

00:22:45.373 --> 00:22:48.220
There hasn't been a press
tour that I have said,

00:22:48.220 --> 00:22:49.670
I'm like, "I love these people."

00:22:49.670 --> 00:22:51.100
I'm lying.
- Yeah.

00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:52.830
- I love these people.

00:22:52.830 --> 00:22:55.167
- How do we know you're not lying?

00:22:55.167 --> 00:22:57.006
(group laughing)
- No, no, because

00:22:57.006 --> 00:22:59.616
I text you, I hug you, like, you know

00:22:59.616 --> 00:23:01.003
how much I love you.
- We still talk-

00:23:01.003 --> 00:23:01.836
- Yeah, we still talk,

00:23:01.836 --> 00:23:02.669
we have a group-
- We still hang out-

00:23:02.669 --> 00:23:04.920
- We have a group chat, I feel comfortable

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:06.010
with these people, these are like

00:23:06.010 --> 00:23:08.317
my secondary family-
- You have a group chat?

00:23:08.317 --> 00:23:10.040
- (group laughing) You
tried to FaceTime us

00:23:10.040 --> 00:23:11.170
the other day on that group chat,

00:23:11.170 --> 00:23:12.817
what are you talking about?

00:23:12.817 --> 00:23:14.340
You're like, "Let's FaceTime."

00:23:14.340 --> 00:23:16.695
And Gloria was like, "Which one of us?"

00:23:16.695 --> 00:23:17.528
(group laughing)
Which one of us in this?

00:23:17.528 --> 00:23:20.230
We're like 15 people in this chat!

00:23:20.230 --> 00:23:22.420
I think we had a blast, and I think,

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:24.920
I just want people to have the best time.

00:23:24.920 --> 00:23:27.950
I think this movie could not
have come at a better time.

00:23:27.950 --> 00:23:29.910
I think the world's crazy out there,

00:23:29.910 --> 00:23:31.900
and I think this movie will allow people

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:35.060
to sort of escape, and have fun,

00:23:35.060 --> 00:23:39.456
while also being taught a
couple lessons along the way.

00:23:39.456 --> 00:23:42.130
'Cause it's so funny, I
think they're gonna see

00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:44.440
what Gaz was talking about in a funny way

00:23:44.440 --> 00:23:46.780
and without realizing just how much

00:23:46.780 --> 00:23:48.990
they're sort of learning
about the dynamics

00:23:48.990 --> 00:23:50.940
in their family, or the generational gaps,

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:53.000
or even the gender roles
in their own family.

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:56.490
I'm really excited for my grandparents,

00:23:56.490 --> 00:23:59.760
and my, well, my grandfather
from my mom's side

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:01.650
and my father to watch this movie.

00:24:01.650 --> 00:24:03.678
You know, I think it'll be great.

00:24:03.678 --> 00:24:05.740
(Shawn laughs)
I think it'll be really fun!

00:24:05.740 --> 00:24:07.150
- I love that, I love that.

00:24:07.150 --> 00:24:09.080
And Matt, Diego, and Gloria, and Andy,

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:11.310
I wanna ask you all a separate question.

00:24:11.310 --> 00:24:12.990
You kind of alluded to it earlier.

00:24:12.990 --> 00:24:14.750
How impactful is that experience?

00:24:14.750 --> 00:24:16.300
That we haven't been in the theater

00:24:16.300 --> 00:24:17.737
for a long time due to COVID,

00:24:17.737 --> 00:24:20.910
and so many changes in our
world that we've known?

00:24:20.910 --> 00:24:23.050
How important and how impactful is it

00:24:23.050 --> 00:24:24.390
to still see it on the theater?

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:25.780
You mentioned like-
- Oh, gosh-

00:24:25.780 --> 00:24:26.613
- When you you saw it in the theater,

00:24:26.613 --> 00:24:27.740
and you saw everybody responding

00:24:27.740 --> 00:24:29.260
to the laughs-
- Yes!

00:24:29.260 --> 00:24:30.440
- Opening it up to you all,

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:32.020
to kind of weigh in-
- It was so special for me,

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:34.840
because that's the reason
that we go to movies,

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:36.477
to have a collective experience.

00:24:36.477 --> 00:24:38.513
And I know, we've all learned how

00:24:38.513 --> 00:24:41.460
to have a collective experience
individually in our homes.

00:24:41.460 --> 00:24:45.010
People have watch parties
through apps and things,

00:24:45.010 --> 00:24:46.760
because we still crave that.

00:24:46.760 --> 00:24:49.570
I love the fact that everything's
accessible from home,

00:24:49.570 --> 00:24:52.640
because who knows, how long
we're gonna be in and out

00:24:52.640 --> 00:24:55.180
of this situation, but
there's nothing like

00:24:55.180 --> 00:24:57.520
seeing bigger than life characters,

00:24:57.520 --> 00:24:59.510
literally bigger than life.

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:02.260
On a screen you see so many subtleties,

00:25:02.260 --> 00:25:06.130
that you'll never catch
on a smaller screen.

00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:09.160
And it's just being together
which is what I hope,

00:25:09.160 --> 00:25:10.210
this film does-
- You don't pick up-

00:25:10.210 --> 00:25:11.265
- Like families-
- Your phone,

00:25:11.265 --> 00:25:12.849
you don't pick up your phone-
- Right.

00:25:12.849 --> 00:25:13.682
- When you're watching-
- Oh, wait a minute,

00:25:13.682 --> 00:25:15.540
let me text somebody while I'm doing this.

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:16.400
Or me, let me play

00:25:16.400 --> 00:25:17.460
Candy Crush.
- Can you rewind?

00:25:17.460 --> 00:25:20.000
(group laughing)
- There's no pause!

00:25:20.000 --> 00:25:21.964
- Also laugh is so contagious.

00:25:21.964 --> 00:25:23.390
- Yeah.
- Like someone laughs

00:25:23.390 --> 00:25:24.450
and it just makes you wanna,

00:25:24.450 --> 00:25:27.640
it's such an empathetic
experience to go to the cinema.

00:25:27.640 --> 00:25:29.380
Like you said, you wouldn't
pull out your phone

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:31.410
because you're concerned,
that you might ruin

00:25:31.410 --> 00:25:33.340
someone else's experience
by shining your light.

00:25:33.340 --> 00:25:35.300
Or like, if someone you know next to you

00:25:35.300 --> 00:25:38.290
is sort of feeling that moment,
you're like, "Oh, wait."

00:25:38.290 --> 00:25:39.850
It becomes like this empathetic

00:25:39.850 --> 00:25:42.300
group experience.
- Or when someone belly laughs

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:43.970
at something totally unexpected,

00:25:43.970 --> 00:25:45.240
then you're going, "Wait a minute."

00:25:45.240 --> 00:25:47.550
- What did I miss?
- What is going on there?

00:25:47.550 --> 00:25:49.993
It's just beautiful, together, you know?

00:25:49.993 --> 00:25:53.930
- Or cries, and you go, "Are
you crying?" (Gloria laughs)

00:25:53.930 --> 00:25:55.220
- Matt and Diego, I'm curious from you all

00:25:55.220 --> 00:25:56.610
about that theatrical experience.

00:25:56.610 --> 00:25:58.900
Being in it, and seeing
it on the big screen.

00:25:58.900 --> 00:26:01.610
- I mean, man, seeing it
yesterday with an audience

00:26:01.610 --> 00:26:04.720
was amazing, 'cause you
could really see, and hear,

00:26:04.720 --> 00:26:07.410
like, and be with the audience
as they were laughing,

00:26:07.410 --> 00:26:09.900
as they were gasping, as they were crying.

00:26:09.900 --> 00:26:12.850
You know, I saw it with
my mom, and my mom was,

00:26:12.850 --> 00:26:16.120
the third act in she was just
crying and then laughing,

00:26:16.120 --> 00:26:19.260
and you know, there's
really nothing like it.

00:26:19.260 --> 00:26:22.360
There's nothing like it,
it was amazing to be able

00:26:22.360 --> 00:26:26.570
to be in the theater
yesterday and see how excited,

00:26:26.570 --> 00:26:30.702
and how in it the audience
members were, you know?

00:26:30.702 --> 00:26:33.690
It was great.
- And those are the reactions,

00:26:33.690 --> 00:26:37.220
'cause whenever I do a
concert, I know immediately

00:26:37.220 --> 00:26:40.210
how that audience is
responding 'cause I'm there.

00:26:40.210 --> 00:26:42.760
- Yeah.
- But actors do their craft

00:26:42.760 --> 00:26:44.450
and hope for the best.

00:26:44.450 --> 00:26:47.640
And hope that what they do has translated

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:49.940
to the point where it's gonna move people.

00:26:49.940 --> 00:26:52.470
And in the theater is
where you see it happen.

00:26:52.470 --> 00:26:54.340
- You sort of get instant gratification.

00:26:54.340 --> 00:26:56.530
- Yes, you don't see it in people's homes,

00:26:56.530 --> 00:26:58.880
they're all doing their thing, you know?

00:26:58.880 --> 00:27:02.150
But it's an amazing, oh, my God, it was

00:27:02.150 --> 00:27:04.300
so special for me.
- Matt for you, I am curious.

00:27:04.300 --> 00:27:05.320
- Completely concur.

00:27:05.320 --> 00:27:07.660
I've probably seen the
film five or six times.

00:27:07.660 --> 00:27:10.400
That was the first time in
front of a live audience.

00:27:10.400 --> 00:27:12.850
And, there's just an energy to it,

00:27:12.850 --> 00:27:17.340
and people grabbing me
after, like, who were,

00:27:17.340 --> 00:27:18.670
you can't judge a book by its cover.

00:27:18.670 --> 00:27:20.090
These were not Latino people,

00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:22.067
who were just like, "I cried
three times during that movie,

00:27:22.067 --> 00:27:23.480
"and I didn't expect it."

00:27:23.480 --> 00:27:26.300
And how often I heard a version

00:27:26.300 --> 00:27:28.290
at the party after the screening,

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:30.337
how often I heard a version of like,

00:27:30.337 --> 00:27:31.897
"We need this movie.

00:27:31.897 --> 00:27:33.670
"We need this movie right now."

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:36.460
And another thing I heard was like,

00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:38.037
and it was almost evenly split,

00:27:38.037 --> 00:27:40.190
"I can't wait to show my kids,"

00:27:40.190 --> 00:27:42.810
and then, "I can't wait
to show my parents,"

00:27:42.810 --> 00:27:46.340
which I think speaks
to, you used the word,

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:48.490
universality of this.

00:27:48.490 --> 00:27:50.453
It's a film that I think,

00:27:51.440 --> 00:27:56.070
works because, at the same time
there's a great specificity

00:27:56.070 --> 00:27:58.580
to the humor, and you hit on it.

00:27:58.580 --> 00:28:01.800
It's getting better, but many depictions

00:28:01.800 --> 00:28:03.530
in film and television of Latinos,

00:28:03.530 --> 00:28:05.450
it's frequently we're a monolith,

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:07.790
we're a uni culture, and we're not.

00:28:07.790 --> 00:28:11.580
And years ago, you know, I was
an executive at DreamWorks.

00:28:11.580 --> 00:28:13.737
I had a whole other career,

00:28:13.737 --> 00:28:16.490
and they gave us lunch downstairs.

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:19.310
And my assistant came
up one day and she said,

00:28:19.310 --> 00:28:20.290
she didn't mean anything by it,

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:22.580
she's like, "Oh, they have
your people's food downstairs."

00:28:22.580 --> 00:28:24.520
And I thought to myself,
if I go down there

00:28:24.520 --> 00:28:26.720
and there's papa rellena,
I'm gonna be shocked.

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:28.860
So I go down there, and it was,

00:28:28.860 --> 00:28:31.370
here's the punchline, it was tostada bar.

00:28:31.370 --> 00:28:33.610
I was like, "Okay, she
thought it was Mexican food."

00:28:33.610 --> 00:28:34.990
By the way, that's American food.

00:28:34.990 --> 00:28:36.770
- Yeah, exactly.
- Yeah.

00:28:36.770 --> 00:28:37.930
- Yes.
- You know?

00:28:37.930 --> 00:28:39.343
And we wanted to,

00:28:40.200 --> 00:28:43.500
on the hand, celebrate the differences

00:28:43.500 --> 00:28:46.900
between these cultures, but
what I love about the movie,

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:49.820
and especially the end
is, at the end of the day

00:28:49.820 --> 00:28:51.780
there are these commonalities though.

00:28:51.780 --> 00:28:54.380
There are these common
denominators of family,

00:28:54.380 --> 00:28:58.280
of food, of music, of closeness,

00:28:58.280 --> 00:28:59.670
that transcend culture.

00:28:59.670 --> 00:29:02.530
And, you can be an immigrant from Cuba,

00:29:02.530 --> 00:29:04.520
or you can be an immigrant from Senegal,

00:29:04.520 --> 00:29:07.748
and, you know what that is,

00:29:07.748 --> 00:29:10.820
to on the one hand, you come to America

00:29:10.820 --> 00:29:13.840
and there's this beautiful
assimilation that takes place,

00:29:13.840 --> 00:29:15.900
but there's a cost to the assimilation.

00:29:15.900 --> 00:29:18.490
And that tension of like how do we hold on

00:29:18.490 --> 00:29:20.710
to these things that are so dear,

00:29:20.710 --> 00:29:23.150
and are so part of who we are,

00:29:23.150 --> 00:29:26.350
but at the same time
join this melting pot?

00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:29.760
And seeing the audience validate that

00:29:29.760 --> 00:29:31.477
was just really exciting.
- Yeah.

00:29:31.477 --> 00:29:33.150
- That was beautifully put.

00:29:33.150 --> 00:29:34.760
Before I wrap this conversation,

00:29:34.760 --> 00:29:36.530
'cause I heard you talking
about it a little bit earlier,

00:29:36.530 --> 00:29:39.330
I have to ask, what is the scene,

00:29:39.330 --> 00:29:42.380
that didn't make it into
the film, (Matt laughs)

00:29:42.380 --> 00:29:45.230
that you all thought would make,

00:29:45.230 --> 00:29:46.390
God, that was an amazing scene!

00:29:46.390 --> 00:29:48.140
Or you just had fun filming it.

00:29:48.140 --> 00:29:50.240
- So many.
- The wedding was amazing.

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:52.049
I mean we had so much fun-
- But that's in the movie-

00:29:52.049 --> 00:29:53.825
- Shooting that wedding-
- But what was-

00:29:53.825 --> 00:29:56.450
- Like what's the scene
that wasn't in there?

00:29:56.450 --> 00:29:58.090
You said you went through different edits.

00:29:58.090 --> 00:29:59.890
- No, everything we wanted stayed there.

00:29:59.890 --> 00:30:01.143
- Everything is is in there-
- Everything we wanted-

00:30:01.143 --> 00:30:04.140
- There's one scene between me and Gloria,

00:30:04.140 --> 00:30:05.770
where I said, "I've tried my best."

00:30:05.770 --> 00:30:07.567
- Yeah.
- And she said,

00:30:07.567 --> 00:30:10.220
"I know you have, Billy,"
and she gives me a kiss.

00:30:10.220 --> 00:30:11.980
It was early when we
put the kids to sleep,

00:30:11.980 --> 00:30:13.410
After we're in bed together.
- Yeah-

00:30:13.410 --> 00:30:15.020
- But I understood why they don't-

00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:16.570
- No, yeah-
- Wanna pay off-

00:30:16.570 --> 00:30:17.600
- it was too soon-

00:30:17.600 --> 00:30:18.433
You saw there were gonna get-
- Putting us back

00:30:18.433 --> 00:30:20.060
together early.
- Yeah.

00:30:20.060 --> 00:30:22.395
- It was leaning-
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, it was-

00:30:22.395 --> 00:30:23.620
- Structurally it was off, you know?

00:30:23.620 --> 00:30:25.820
- It was just lines that I missed, jokes,

00:30:25.820 --> 00:30:27.980
and by the way, it was Ruben Rabasa-

00:30:27.980 --> 00:30:30.196
- Acapulco-
- Who steals every scene.

00:30:30.196 --> 00:30:31.726
- Acapulco-
- That is the one!

00:30:31.726 --> 00:30:32.559
- Acapulco is the one-
- Acapulco is the one!

00:30:32.559 --> 00:30:34.903
- That dinner table scene
which we shot for two days,

00:30:38.190 --> 00:30:42.220
we couldn't get through, some
of the lines because he was,

00:30:42.220 --> 00:30:45.090
thankfully you let us,
you gave us the blessing

00:30:45.090 --> 00:30:49.470
of being able to you know,
improvise and do fun stuff.

00:30:49.470 --> 00:30:52.350
But Ruben Rabasa, oh my God, it was like,

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:55.920
we were just cracking up for
this stuff he would throw out.

00:30:55.920 --> 00:30:57.050
And Chloe.

00:30:57.050 --> 00:30:58.983
- Yeah, and Chloe-
- Chloe and Casey?

00:30:58.983 --> 00:31:00.960
- And Casey!
- They would go off,

00:31:00.960 --> 00:31:04.020
and Gaz would say, "Just go."

00:31:04.020 --> 00:31:06.000
Their improvisations,

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:07.450
were so insane.

00:31:07.450 --> 00:31:10.650
I missed that her she-boss didn't make it.

00:31:10.650 --> 00:31:12.267
- I saved her-
- You saved her.

00:31:12.267 --> 00:31:13.907
- We kept it for Andy.
- You kept it for Andy,

00:31:13.907 --> 00:31:16.310
and people cracked up when you said it!

00:31:16.310 --> 00:31:18.510
Because it was so out
of character (laughs)

00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:20.800
for you to say the she-boss.

00:31:20.800 --> 00:31:23.050
But some of the stuff
that they came up with,

00:31:23.050 --> 00:31:24.250
oh my Lord!
- She has a video.

00:31:24.250 --> 00:31:26.080
- Yeah, I have a video of that scene

00:31:26.080 --> 00:31:28.660
where Ruben said "Acapulco,"

00:31:28.660 --> 00:31:30.470
and the entire

00:31:31.440 --> 00:31:33.260
set, fell apart.

00:31:33.260 --> 00:31:35.410
When I say fell apart,
I mean the camera guy

00:31:35.410 --> 00:31:37.610
was like, "I can't even
hold this camera anymore."

00:31:37.610 --> 00:31:41.400
Dying laughing, Gaz came
in like, dying laughing,

00:31:41.400 --> 00:31:43.260
but also like, "Guys, we gotta film."

00:31:43.260 --> 00:31:45.050
And then the more he heard us laugh,

00:31:45.050 --> 00:31:46.977
the more he was laughing, and he was like,

00:31:46.977 --> 00:31:50.080
"I can't stop this," and
Andy, and I'm laughing,

00:31:50.080 --> 00:31:53.610
and you hear me and Andy
is just cracking up,

00:31:53.610 --> 00:31:55.760
and he can't even breathe.
- I was crying.

00:31:55.760 --> 00:31:57.610
- He was crying, he was like cleaning up,

00:31:57.610 --> 00:31:59.750
and then he kept putting
a napkin on his head,

00:31:59.750 --> 00:32:01.570
like just giving up on life.

00:32:01.570 --> 00:32:02.680
It was so funny,

00:32:02.680 --> 00:32:04.060
I have a video-
- How did you,

00:32:04.060 --> 00:32:06.350
you had a camera and you
started to videotape me?

00:32:06.350 --> 00:32:08.408
- Yeah, I just started
filming you! (laughing)

00:32:08.408 --> 00:32:09.241
- I was like-
- I was off camera,

00:32:09.241 --> 00:32:10.487
I wasn't on camera!
- Oh, that was-

00:32:10.487 --> 00:32:12.850
- It was a reaction to, probably Acapulco!

00:32:12.850 --> 00:32:14.960
- Because it was like the
third time that happened.

00:32:14.960 --> 00:32:17.620
Like we would finish the scene,
and then it would be like,

00:32:17.620 --> 00:32:20.698
or like, we're about to
finish the scene, Acapulco!

00:32:20.698 --> 00:32:21.531
And he would die!

00:32:21.531 --> 00:32:22.364
Second time.

00:32:22.364 --> 00:32:23.897
Third time, we were like, "You guys,

00:32:23.897 --> 00:32:25.580
"we need a 20-minute laugh break,

00:32:25.580 --> 00:32:27.132
because we can't!"
- That's the one line

00:32:27.132 --> 00:32:28.070
I regretted in the movie-
- He was reminiscing,

00:32:28.070 --> 00:32:30.259
about an Acapulco experience-
- Acapulco-

00:32:30.259 --> 00:32:31.350
- And when we're talking about,

00:32:31.350 --> 00:32:34.123
her taking off to Mexico-
- Director's cut.

00:32:35.010 --> 00:32:36.453
- After this whole speech, that is like,

00:32:36.453 --> 00:32:40.000
I don't even know how long,
and then all of a sudden,

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:44.000
I say, "I'm going to Mexico,"
and he goes, "Oooh, Acapulco!"

00:32:44.000 --> 00:32:47.230
I mean, and just the way he
said it, it was so funny.

00:32:47.230 --> 00:32:48.920
And I also have a picture-
- Lewd and lascivious,

00:32:48.920 --> 00:32:50.710
as I was saying-
- I have a picture of you

00:32:50.710 --> 00:32:52.700
that day that you were
playing with your food,

00:32:52.700 --> 00:32:53.750
and it's the cutest picture.

00:32:53.750 --> 00:32:56.710
You made a smiley face with
your frijoles! (laughs)

00:32:56.710 --> 00:32:58.900
- Okay, so I need to see this video

00:32:58.900 --> 00:33:00.177
after we're done wrapping.
- Yes, yes!

00:33:00.177 --> 00:33:02.060
- And I need to see that with her frijoles

00:33:02.060 --> 00:33:02.893
in there.
- Oh my God!

00:33:02.893 --> 00:33:04.997
- And we need a director's cut, clearly.

00:33:04.997 --> 00:33:07.035
- We need the Acapulco scene-
- We need the Acapulco scene!

00:33:07.035 --> 00:33:09.640
- Yes!
- But honestly, as a consumer,

00:33:09.640 --> 00:33:13.620
and as somebody who wasn't
on set, those laughs,

00:33:13.620 --> 00:33:16.430
the heart, the feeling, the
intentionality of like, family,

00:33:16.430 --> 00:33:18.670
the perceived division that became unity

00:33:18.670 --> 00:33:20.530
towards the end, beautiful!

00:33:20.530 --> 00:33:22.830
I appreciated it, and
thank you all so much.

00:33:22.830 --> 00:33:24.947
- And she said it, "Actors may lie,

00:33:24.947 --> 00:33:26.650
"but you can't fake real."
- Yeah.

00:33:26.650 --> 00:33:28.950
- And that energy gets on the project.

00:33:28.950 --> 00:33:30.693
- Yeah-
- Whether it's music, or-

00:33:31.560 --> 00:33:33.670
- So, I was not on their set.

00:33:33.670 --> 00:33:36.240
Ironically enough, I was 10 miles away

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:37.520
on the set of a television show

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:39.850
that I created and was producing.

00:33:39.850 --> 00:33:42.920
And, it was mostly a Latino
cast, Latino filmmakers,

00:33:42.920 --> 00:33:45.020
and we called ourselves, La Familia.

00:33:45.020 --> 00:33:46.530
And it was real.

00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:50.640
It was that instant, like
from the moment we met.

00:33:50.640 --> 00:33:54.040
And, I met most of you, really last night,

00:33:54.040 --> 00:33:56.660
for the very first time,
and I was around you

00:33:56.660 --> 00:34:00.239
for five minutes, and I
was like, that's a Familia.

00:34:00.239 --> 00:34:03.130
I was like, I understand this, I know it,

00:34:03.130 --> 00:34:05.030
it was very heartwarming to see.

00:34:05.030 --> 00:34:07.702
And so as a latecomer to the party,

00:34:07.702 --> 00:34:10.430
(chuckles) thank you guys
for being so welcoming-

00:34:10.430 --> 00:34:14.107
- Welcome to the party!
(group laughing and clapping)

00:34:14.107 --> 00:34:17.310
- But Gloria, you said it, it cannot be,

00:34:17.310 --> 00:34:19.940
and what's amazing is, it's on the screen.

00:34:19.940 --> 00:34:21.590
- Yeah!
- You see it.

00:34:21.590 --> 00:34:24.430
It's a cohesiveness, it's a family thing,

00:34:24.430 --> 00:34:26.490
and I'm not surprised that you created it.

00:34:26.490 --> 00:34:29.190
Knowing you, I was like,
"Oh, good on them."

00:34:29.190 --> 00:34:30.520
Like, that made me so happy.
- Well,

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:34.320
I mean the thing is that
we were all in Atlanta,

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:35.993
for eight weeks.

00:34:37.204 --> 00:34:41.460
We didn't have a real in-point
to Atlanta when we got there,

00:34:41.460 --> 00:34:46.460
and everyone had girlfriends,
husbands, wife and kids,

00:34:47.350 --> 00:34:48.823
family-
- Daughter

00:34:48.823 --> 00:34:50.430
and grandkids-
- Daughter, grandkids,

00:34:50.430 --> 00:34:52.310
like, when we were not shooting,

00:34:52.310 --> 00:34:56.340
when anyone was not shooting,
they were hanging out, no?

00:34:56.340 --> 00:35:01.000
And then, Enrique Murciano
threw a party in his house,

00:35:01.000 --> 00:35:04.990
the guy who plays Junior,
for his birthday, Saturday,

00:35:04.990 --> 00:35:07.350
and Diego brings a friend that cooks like

00:35:07.350 --> 00:35:09.310
the best burgers in
Mexico, (Matt laughing)

00:35:09.310 --> 00:35:11.720
and we're just dancing
like until 3:00 a.m.

00:35:11.720 --> 00:35:13.638
- And I made my cosmos-
- See, and-

00:35:13.638 --> 00:35:15.910
- It's very good!
- She made her cosmos, no?

00:35:15.910 --> 00:35:17.150
- I brought some Inca balloons-

00:35:17.150 --> 00:35:20.420
- Then some night Adria
takes us all out bowling,

00:35:20.420 --> 00:35:22.780
and Diego starts singing "Journey"

00:35:22.780 --> 00:35:23.980
in the bowling alley. (Adria laughs)

00:35:23.980 --> 00:35:26.060
- And we were dancing with
that band that was there-

00:35:26.060 --> 00:35:28.620
- And that the wrap party that Gloria

00:35:28.620 --> 00:35:29.900
threw at the house-
- Yes, oh, at the house,

00:35:29.900 --> 00:35:31.710
in Miami!
- Oh, my God, were you-

00:35:31.710 --> 00:35:34.420
- Yeah, she invited the entire
crew, everyone was there.

00:35:34.420 --> 00:35:36.910
I mean, no one does that, really, I mean,

00:35:36.910 --> 00:35:39.840
that party was legendary.
- That was a big party.

00:35:39.840 --> 00:35:41.400
- Legendary!
- I found,

00:35:41.400 --> 00:35:43.695
lots of interesting things in my house.

00:35:43.695 --> 00:35:45.946
(group laughing)
After that party!

00:35:45.946 --> 00:35:50.118
Some people called to ask for
them, and some people didn't!

00:35:50.118 --> 00:35:51.880
(group laughing and clapping)
Just left it!

00:35:51.880 --> 00:35:55.530
- We're gonna get that
definitely in the next interview,

00:35:55.530 --> 00:35:57.600
but thank you for watching Academy Panels,

00:35:57.600 --> 00:35:58.680
my name is Shawn Finnie,

00:35:58.680 --> 00:36:01.153
and make sure you go and check
out "Father of the Bride".

00:36:01.153 --> 00:36:04.486
(upbeat exciting music)

